From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:17:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 355/488] DeepSeek-V4.1: mHC post rounds the first stream's
 product, as TileKernels does

The standalone post differed from TileKernels' mhc_post_fwd in about 1 of
70,000 values (GB10, image spark-ds41f:19f2c20ed4d6-next-4). TileKernels writes
x_local = c * d; x_local += a[m] * b[m]. Of the first addition's two products
nvcc fuses the left one, so its PTX for every output (all 96 chains of a
thread, lowered with the deployed TileLang's nvcc flags for sm_121a) is

    mul.f32     comb[0, n] * residual[0]
    fma.rn.f32  post[n], x, .
    fma.rn.f32  comb[m, n], residual[m], .    m = 1..3
    cvt.rn.bf16.f32

The port rounded post[n] * x instead (mul.rn.f32) and fused all four stream
products. post_streams now writes TileKernels' sequence: the first stream's
product, then T.fma for x and streams 1 to 3. Its PTX is TileKernels' to the
instruction and operand (mul.f32, four fma.rn.f32, no ftz), with the same
instruction count and registers as before.

The fused post_pre entry already compiled to that sequence, by the same
contraction of the same source shape. It now writes it with T.fma as well, so
the standalone and fused posts no longer depend on nvcc's choice of product;
its PTX keeps the same operations and register count.

test_post_order_of_operations encoded the port's order: TileKernels gives
eps, not eps (1 + 2^-7), for token 1's output 0 (5,120 values differed). Its
docstring and expected values now state TileKernels' arithmetic, and output 1
of token 1 adds the case that tells a fused post[n] * x from a rounded one.
